\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Currency \highlight\uc2  Shrugs Off  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Closes Stronger\b0\par\par\uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 21:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real closes stronger despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverse-swap auction\par \par \uc2 --Real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen stabilizing in range between BRL1.80 and BRL1.82\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL1.8107 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeff Fick \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closed stronger Tuesday despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the real tracked overseas gains against the U.S. dollar by other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on expectations for greater liquidity in global capital markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.8107 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was stronger from Monday's close at BRL1.8134 to the greenback and represented a sharp reversal from the weaker opening quote of BRL1.8193.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market buckled early under the weight of expectations for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which had announced a reverse-swap auction late Monday to roll over contracts coming due April 2. Players have paid closer attention recently to the increased rhetoric coming from government officials that has indicated Brazil is comfortable with the real trading at the level of BRL1.80 to the dollar. That likely means a continuing firm stance against short-term investment inflows and the possibility of capital outflows in the future, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was basically stable. That's the trend now for the foreseeable future," said Wilson Menezes, a trader at Sao Paulo-based brokerage Novo Mundo. The real will likely remain trading rangebound between BRL1.80 and BRL1.82 before ending the year at BRL1.85 to the greenback, Menezes said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real's early session weakness faded after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  carried out its reverse-swap auction, selling 41,200 contracts worth $2.1 billion at two maturities, on June 1 and July 2. The auctions, which allow investors to swap \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positions for government paper linked to interest rates, typically support the dollar by removing Brazilian real exposure from the market. The auctions were used extensively in late 2005 and early 2006 before market interest in the swaps waned. The reverse swaps have been used infrequently since then.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market also took direction Tuesday from weak economic data in the U.S., where the troubled housing sector continued to show declines in home prices in January. U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke, meanwhile, also continued to indicate that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's easy money policies will continue into the future. There also continues to be speculation about a third asset-purchase program, or QE3.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While Brazil's towering interest rates have made the country a favored destination for investors seeking higher rates of return, the government's recent measures to tamp down speculative inflows are working--perhaps too well, said one trader.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're going to have lower inflows," said Sidnei Nehme, director at the NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo. Foreign direct investment and exports are falling behind last year's tally, while the implementation of a financial transactions tax on overseas loans of less than six years was also affecting the flow of dollars into Brazil, Nehme noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The great risk is if we end up with net dollar outflows, which will affect the ability to finance the current account deficit," Nehme said. Brazil expects to post a current account deficit of about $68 billion in 2012. "We may not have enough dollars [entering the country] to finance this $70 billion. The government would then need to use part of its reserves," Nehme said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real is likely headed for a long and subtle weakening to about BRL2.00 to the dollar by the third quarter, according to Nehme. Previously, the trader expected the real to reach that mark by the end of the year. "This is going to happen gradually because flows are going to turn negative," Nehme said. "The government will have to let the real weaken or mess with the measures it's implemented."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085, Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 27-03-12 1932GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120327e83r0015s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
