\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  to keep  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 -Mantega\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 18:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finance Minister Mantega says steps to protect industry\par \par \uc2 * Vows to keep fighting excess inflows, strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Says renewal of power licenses offers chance to cut rates\par \par \uc2 (Add details on potential measures, comments, background throughout)\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, March 22 (Reuters) - The Brazilian government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to limit a rally in the real that risks derailing an incipient economic recovery and hampering manufacturers, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A series of measures including tax hikes and interest-rate reductions have helped pare gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now at a "more favorable" level than a year ago, Mantega told reporters after a meeting with business leaders in Brasilia.\par \par \uc2 "The real can't gain further ground or else our exports will turn even less competitive," Mantega said "We will keep with our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - that is a government pledge."\par \par \uc2 Mantega's remarks signal that the government remains convinced that a recent decline in manufacturing activity in Latin America's economy is more a function of unfair trade practices by the world's richest countries -- whose economies are struggling.\par \par \uc2 "There is a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy being implemented by some countries, and that is what puts Brazilian manufacturers in a disadvantageous position," Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 Yet, local business leaders say that high taxes and labor costs, outdated legislation and an aging infrastructure are more to blame for Brazil's flagging industry than external factors.\par \par \uc2 The real weakened for a fourth day on Thursday, shedding 0.3 percent to a bid price of 1.8242 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained 2.2 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 ELECTRICITY RATES\par \par \uc2 The government is considering additional measures to lower financing costs for companies, which, along with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate cuts, will ease the burden on exporters and local manufacturers whose goods compete with imports locally, Mantega said. He did not elaborate on what measures were being considered.\par \par \uc2 He said logistics costs are too high in Brazil and that the government was analyzing ways to bring them down. At the meeting, according to Mantega, entrepreneurs complained about the high cost of power rates and transport bottlenecks as issues that increase their costs.\par \par \uc2 Mantega said the government was expected to decide whether or not to renew more than 60 percent of existing licenses for electricity generation, transmission and distribution -- and should take advantage of the process to push rates lower.\par \par \uc2 He added that the process should also allow the government to end a longstanding link between power rates and inflation indexes.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 1.824 reais)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Guillermo Parra-Bernal, Brad Haynes and Frederico Rosas; Additional reporting by Patricia Duarte in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo; Editing by Andrea Evans)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 BRAZIL\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 -MANTEGA (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U   \par \