\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  fall as China manufacturing slows\b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 15:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real falls to lowest since Jan. 9; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen\par \par \uc2 * Chile peso sheds 0.74 pct; top export copper at two-week low\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso off 0.69 pct, real ends down 0.18 pct\par \par \uc2 By Caroline Stauffer\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 22 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on Thursday as faltering factory activity in China cast doubt on the Asian giant's appetite for the region's commodities and surprisingly weak European data added to global growth fears.\par \par \uc2 China's industrial activity fell for the fifth straight month in March, a purchasing managers index showed.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, German and French manufacturing suffered sharp declines in March that even the most pessimistic economists had failed to predict, according to PMI data..\par \par \uc2 "When there's negative news out of China, Europe or the United States you have this risk-off trade," said Kathryn Rooney Vera, an emerging markets analyst at Bulltick Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Bad news out of China pushes Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker because China is a huge commodity consumer - that's the main driver here," she said.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso shed 0.74 percent to bid 487.2 per dollar. It was the peso's weakest close in two weeks and its biggest single-day drop since March 6.\par \par \uc2 Copper prices fell to a two-week low on expectations of lower demand from China, the world's biggest metals consumer, and Europe. Chile is the world's top copper exporter, and its mines produce 30 percent of the world's copper supply.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real weakened for a fourth day, losing 0.18 percent to bid 1.8207 per dollar. Declining industry performance in China, Brazil's top trading partner, led to the real's weakest close since Jan. 9.\par \par \uc2 Still, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the real from gaining ground as it tries to maintain the competitiveness of Brazil's already expensive exports and spur economic growth. .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars on Feb. 6 for the first time since September of last year. The real has weakened 5.5 percent since it waded back into the market.\par \par \uc2 "They are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1.8 to stop the real's momentum, but I have a hard time, barring a significant global situation that would impact all of Latin America, seeing it going above 1.9," said Eduardo Suarez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital.\par \par \uc2 Though analysts doubt the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will drastically weaken the real, the efforts have led some to recommend the region's other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Mantega and (President Dilma) Rousseff have made no bones about their intention to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and for that reason we aren't extremely bullish on the real. ... The Finance Ministry should not be dictating monetary policy, but that's what you see going on here," said Bulltick's Rooney Vera, who favors the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 PESO RESPECTS MOVING AVERAGE\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly weakened despite signs of recovery in the United States, the world's largest economy. New U.S. claims for unemployment benefits dropped to a four-year low last week, a government report said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, which sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to the United States, saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken 0.69 percent, to 12.7948 on risk aversion from the Chinese and European data.\par \par \uc2 But traders said the peso isn't likely to change its appreciation tendency because the Latin America's only fully convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed below an important technical signal.\par \par \uc2 "I think the key resistance is the 200-day moving average, which is 12.935," said Alfredo Puig, a trader at Vector brokerage in Monterrey. "There's been resistance at 12.85, and I think there will be at 12.90 as well."\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso strengthened 0.08 percent to 1,759.7 after the country posted 6.1 percent growth in the last quarter of 2011 versus the year-earlier period. Foreign investment and expanding consumer credit helped shield Colombia from global economic troubles.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | ABX | BNX | FUN | U | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120325e83m000ab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
