\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Worries, Interest Rates\b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 20:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real exits active trading at BRL1.7820 Vs BRL1.7693 Thursday\par \par \uc2 --Market worries persist on possible government steps to stem inflows\par \par \uc2 --Inflation data underscores long-term trend toward lower base rate\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Murphy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real ended active trading weaker against the U.S. dollar Friday as investors continued to fret over possible government action to stem investment inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real ended regular trading at BRL1.7820 to the dollar, weaker than Thursday's BRL1.7693, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the sixth straight session for the real to depreciate against the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government officials have come out with almost daily statements saying they could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market in order to force a depreciation of the real," said Olvidio Soares, a trader at Sao Paulo's Interbolsa brokerage. "Additional taxes on inflows are likely to be the government's weapon of choice."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Guido Mantega himself has led the chorus of officials proclaiming the need to protect Brazilian exporters and manufacturers from what he has called a global "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war." In recent comments, Mantega has said Brazil's government reserves the right to adopt measures restraining short-term investment inflows as a way to reduce the appreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All of this has led to a somewhat speculative market," said Soares. "All it takes is a market rumor on possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and investors start buying dollars."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, Friday's session did not even see the more traditional form of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by means of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar purchases via snap auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Current levels for the real against the dollar seem agreeable to the government," said Hencorp Commcor Brokerage trader Luiz Henrique. "In recent days, with the real at this level, the government has held off from any market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prospects for lower interest rates are also helping depreciate the real. High Brazilian interest rates have served as a consistent lure for foreign investment inflows, a major reason the government has targeted them in the past for higher taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But earlier this week, Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  signaled a more aggressive policy of interest rate reductions by cutting its Selic base rate three-quarters-of-a-point to 9.75%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most analysts expect more rate cuts to come, undermining the attractiveness of Brazilian fixed-income investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In trading on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange on Friday, investors ratified the idea of more cuts in the months to come with the base rate ending 2012 between 8.5% and 8.75%. The January 1, 2013 interest rate futures contract closed at 8.65%, down a tick from 8.66% Thursday. The contracts reflect investor expectations for annualized interest rates at future dates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation data earlier Friday tended to underscore prospects for more rate cuts. Official February data showed Brazil's 12-month inflation rate declining to 5.84% from 6.22% as of January. Lower inflation should encourage the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to speed up rate cuts, according to analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger U.S. dollar overseas also contributed to the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness, traders said. The dollar gained against the euro and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on strong U.S. jobs data, released earlier Friday. The U.S. created 227,000 jobs in February, higher than the 213,000 predicted by analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-07090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 09-03-12 1952GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120309e8390017p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
