\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real hits 8-week low as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 20:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Speculation of sovereign wealth fund dollar spurs selling\par \par \uc2 * Real likely to keep lagging region-wide rally\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real slumps 1.29 pct, Mexico peso up 0.34 pct\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 9 (Reuters) - Brazil's real slumped to an eight-week low on Friday on expectations of more big interest rate cuts and new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures by authorities worried that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is crimping growth.\par \par \uc2 The real slumped by the most this week since the beginning of November when it was hit by panic about European banks, shedding 3.3 percent against the dollar and leading losses in March for the 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Speculation that Brazil's sovereign wealth fund could soon start to buy dollars in the market spurred selling. Authorities have deployed a growing tool chest of measures designed to sap the real's strength, which is hurting local industry.\par \par \uc2 Still, analysts said global demand for higher-yielding assets would likely cap losses ahead in the real, which could soon begin appreciating again.\par \par \uc2 A recent liquidity boost of more than half a trillion euros from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to keep driving financial flows into emerging markets offering higher yields, such as Latin America, in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Europe's cheap financing adds to the global pool of money available at low-interest rates that can be invested in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "All this liquidity is the global force that authorities in Brazil are fighting," said Roberto Melzi, a strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The global forces suggest the real could still rally a lot. But all these measures by authorities could make it underperform the rally we are likely to see if money keeps pouring into emerging markets," Melzi said.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the real sank 1.29 percent to 1.7835 to the dollar, paring losses that had taken it to its weakest level since mid-January.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed inflation in Brazil eased in February to its slowest pace for that month in five years, reinforcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's argument for increasingly aggressive interest rate cuts. Lower rates could make investing in Brazilian bonds less attractive.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised analysts by cutting its benchmark interest rate three-quarters of a percentage point to 9.75 percent. After the cut, the median of a Reuters poll showed analysts see the rate falling to 8.5 percent this year, versus a previous floor of 9.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 That would still leave Brazilian interest rates among the highest in the world. Even if the return on fixed-income assets is falling, lower borrowing costs could boost growth and tempt more foreign investors to dive into the country's stock market.\par \par \uc2 Enrique Alvarez, an analyst at IDEAglobal in New York, expects the real to finish the year at about 1.70 to 1.75 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Other major Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose after the U.S. government reported that U.S. employers added more than 200,000 jobs for a third straight month, a sign the world's largest economy was strengthening.\par \par \uc2 Mexico sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to the United States. Mexico's peso gained 0.34 percent to 12.6625 to the dollar for a third day. Earlier it reached 12.6010, its strongest in six months, before pulling back.\par \par \uc2 Rafael Camarena, an economist at Santander in Mexico City, expects the peso to trade laterally between 12.70 and 12.60 per dollar over the next week as investors look for further confirmation that the U.S. economy is improving.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said profit taking could be seen next week after Greece pushed through a bond swap needed to clinch bailout funds.\par \par \uc2 In March the peso's performance has been nearly the opposite that of the real, strengthening nearly 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Alvarez thinks the peso could gain as much as 7 percent yet this year to reach 11.80 to the dollar, helped by investors shifting investment from \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Mexico.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics are pushing more investors to eye the peso, since Mexico is not acting to deter speculators in its market, Alvarez said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen holding its benchmark rate steady at 4.50 percent next week after inflation in February fell back into the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone, below 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso firmed about 0.25 percent to 483.00 to the dollar from 484.40.\par \par \uc2 (Additional Reporting By Jeb Blount in Rio De Janeiro; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | NAT | RBN | E | U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120312e83900176\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
