\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Brazil Will Not Allow  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  To Strengthen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 16:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) Brazil will not allow the real, which gained nearly 10% vs the US dollar to start 2012, to strengthen, pledges Finance Minister Guido Mantega. "Brazil has an arsenal of measures to curb the appreciation of the real," Mantega says. Recent moves included taxes on overseas and trade loans, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  welcomes much-needed foreign investment, "the government will not allow speculative capital inflows," Mantega says. Strong BRL undercuts Brazil's industry, which suffers from cheap imports at home and inability to compete on international markets. Brazil needs stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect industry, Mantega adds. (Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:04 (Dow Jones) Shares of Giant Interactive (GA) are likely to rise over the next two months, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in the wake of the Chinese online-gaming company's upbeat 4Q report. The investment bank calls GA undervalued as it only trades at 6 times earnings. Meanwhile, Roth Capital boosts its price target $1 to $7 following last week's upgrade to buy. It also boost estimates on GA while noting "we are pleased to see the company taking aggressive steps to increase its presence in social & webgame market." GA's ADRs rise GA 4.4% to $4.80, hitting a 6-month high this morning. (alexandra.scaggs@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 (Dow Jones) \uc2 AmerisourceBergen\uc2 's (ABC) planned $520M purchase of \uc2 World Courier Group\uc2  suggests the recent, abrupt departure of CFO Michael DiCandilo hasn't weighed on drug wholesaler's deal-making ability. Susquehanna notes this is ABC's fourth acquisition in last six months. The deal, which is seen helping ABC extend its reach for handling more complex "specialty" drugs outside the US, "fits snugly within ABC's stated focus area for M&A," Baird says. ABC up a fraction to $36.63. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:00 (Dow Jones) Guy Haselmann, head of US interest rate strategy at primary dealer \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2 , questions the sustainability of good data from the US, based on better weather and inventory build. China lower growth target is significant, and has clearly affected the commodity complex and hit suppliers such as Australia and Brazil, he says. Meanwhile, Hasselman argues that the EU crisis seems to be worsening, noting that sinking growth is sapping revenues and worsening deficits. "The Euro should start to get hit," he says. "Treasurys are the only logical parking place until uncertainties diminish." He bets benchmark 10-yr yield, recently at 1.95%, to hit 1.90% and then 1.80%, a case he believes could occur this month. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:55 (Dow Jones) E*Trade (ETFC) CFO Matt Audette said the online brokerage has transferred $3B (roughly 23%) of its loan portfolio to "higher quality servicers" that specialize in collecting payments from borrowers that have delinquent loan payments. At a Raymond James investor conference, Audette said the practice over the long term "ultimately will drive down credit costs" for ETFC. The company was hammered by bad bets on the US housing market, and is also mitigating credit losses through its loan modification program. ETFC is targeting re-delinquency rates below 50%, and has posted rates of 29% and 42%, respectively, for its 1-4 family loans and its home equity loans. Shares off 2.3% at $9.48. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:51 (Dow Jones) Bank bonds are "getting crushed" in early trading, says trader Dan Hannis at \uc2 William Blair & Co\uc2 . The risk premium on intermediate term notes from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , \uc2 UBS\uc2 , and \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group\uc2  widened 0.14, 0.22, and 0.35 percentage points, respectively, according to MarketAxess. Shorter-term paper from \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  and \uc2 US Bancorp\uc2  weakened 0.16 and 0.24 points. Hannis doesn't see fall as a buying opportunity. "I'd rather wait for a morning when things are five basis points tighter than catch a falling knife or guess where the bottom might be in a sell-off." (patrick.mcgee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:50 (Dow Jones) Brazil's economy is heating up to start 2012 after struggling in 2H under the weight of weakness in the country's industrial segment, says Finance Minister Guido Mantega, who predicts Latin America's largest economy should grow 4-4.5% in 2012. Last year's GDP increase was 2.7%. "Economic growth will peak in the second half of 2012," he predicts, adding that Brazil "will announce new measures to spur growth as needed." If necessary, they would be aimed at industry. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:51 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  expects small-cap proxy Russell 2000 to pull back 5%-8% in coming months, but rebound before year end. Firm says an above-average number of companies beat 4Q earnings estimates, which spurred the index's 8.5% YTD gain. But now that roughly 75% of companies have reported, "we believe the Russell 2000 is likely to take a well-deserved breather," JPM says. Good economic surprises will likely become more rare, firm adds, which could also serve as a drag on small-cap stocks. "We would like to emphasize that we are not turning negative on small-caps, instead we recommend investors buy quality growth names," JPM adds. (alexandra.scaggs@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:46 (Dow Jones) Brazil seems to be reverting to the "two-speed" pattern of growth seen in 2010 and early 2011 amid strong consumer growth but weak industrial output, says Capital Economics. "The issue going forward is whether or not this is sustainable...we have our doubts." It adds that a resurgence of the euro-zone crisis would mean capital inflows to emerging economies are likely to reverse once again. That, says Capital Economics, can quickly translate into weaker economic performance in Brazil. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:46 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Office Depot\uc2  (ODP) may have a 2012 guidance tweak coming after refinancing debt. ODP this morning unveils private $250M note offering, which will help fund its oversubscribed tender for $250M in 6.23% notes due 2013. No details on new notes' rate, but ODP has been pulling back its interest expense in recent years. Last month, ODP predicted 2012 expense would increase to $80M-$85M from $33.2M in 2011, but CFO \uc2 Michael Newman\uc2  said that projection was placeholder as ODP figures out its next moves on debt. The new notes could be just the thing to remove that placeholder and get a new expense forecast in the lineup. ODP off 2.5% at $3.10 amid broad market declines. (joan.solsman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:42 (Dow Jones) There has been some speculation lately about the potential Cigna (CI) could unload its pharmacy-benefit manager business, which is something CI considered in 2009. CEO David Cordani doesn't definitively say one way or another what CI will do, but speaking at Cowen health-care conference, he says CI " will remain open to structural alternatives that maximize shareholder value so long as they continue to enable me to deliver our promise to our clients and customers." At the same time, he says this is an important business that is "well performing based on a variety of measures." CI down 1% to $43.99. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:41 (Dow Jones) Using liquefied-natural-gas, or LNG, and electricity as fuel for vehicles is a very promising way to moderate China's oil demand, Xizhou Zhou, director at energy consultancy IHS, says at CERA energy conference. Market penetration of these fuels will be successful only if there is enough supply available to supply the huge Chinese market and if the government guarantees competitive prices for producers and consumers. (isabel.ordonez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-06-12 1006ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120306e83600092\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
