\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Brazil Will Not Allow  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  To Strengthen\b0\par\par\uc2 136 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 16:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) Brazil will not allow the real, which gained nearly 10% vs the US dollar to start 2012, to strengthen, pledges Finance Minister Guido Mantega. "Brazil has an arsenal of measures to curb the appreciation of the real," Mantega says. Recent moves included taxes on overseas and trade loans, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  welcomes much-needed foreign investment, "the government will not allow speculative capital inflows," Mantega says. Strong BRL undercuts Brazil's industry, which suffers from cheap imports at home and inability to compete on international markets. Brazil needs stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect industry, Mantega adds. (Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or email john.shipman@dowjones.com [ 03-06-12 1006ET ]\par \par \uc2 80000   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120306e83600091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
