\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Currency \highlight\uc2  Opens Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Rate Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 13:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government weakening real\par \par \uc2 --Expectations for lower interest rates also undermining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real opens trading at BRL1.7325, weaker from Friday's close at BRL1.7278\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Jeff Fick \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market continued to respond Monday to government measures to contain the real's recent strength, with an eye toward an expected interest-rate cut later this week that should further diminish the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's attractiveness to foreign investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real opened at BRL1.7325 to the U.S. dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was weaker from Friday's close at BRL1.7278.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government has increased both the rhetoric and actions against the real's nearly 10% gain against the U.S. dollar so far in 2012, boosting the number of dollar purchases and reverse-swap auctions to sop up excess liquidity as well as implementing measures to cool inflows over the past week. That's sent the real tumbling back from the BRL1.70-to-the-dollar level that appears to be the "line in the sand."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong real is hurting exporters and manufacturers, and Brazil is prepared to use "a large arsenal of weapons" to fight the real's appreciation, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said recently.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mantega has recently reiterated concerns about a "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war" as low interest rates and sluggish economic growth abroad cause foreign investors to seek higher rates of return in Brazil. The key lure are Brazil's towering interest rates, with the Selic base interest rate currently at 10.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meets Tuesday, when its expected to reduce the benchmark Selic rate by at least a half-percentage point. But there is growing evidence that the bank could opt for a more aggressive rate cut that would further decrease investor interest in Brazil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on overnight futures contracts point to expectations by some that policy makers could slash rates by 75 basis points. That would accelerate the series of half-point cuts that initially started in August, when central bankers were concerned that fallout from the ongoing European debt crisis could affect activity in Latin America's largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's interest rates have made it a favored destination for the carry trade, with investors borrowing cheap cash in the U.S. and Japan, where interest rates are near zero, and then investing the proceeds in emerging-market countries such as Brazil, where returns are high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lower interest rates typically cause demand for assets to wane, which could give the real room to weaken, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a recent research note. "This hectic behavior increases the chances of breaking the topside of the BRL1.70-BRL1.75 range," \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategists said. "A Selic cut greater than 0.50 percentage point could push [the real] to BRL1.80."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But central bankers also face the real danger that the recent surge in inflows could just as quickly disappear should the scenario in Europe worsen. Capital Economics, for example, predicts the rapid inflows into Brazil won't continue unabated throughout 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given that we expect the euro-zone debt crisis to deepen this year, the severe financial dislocation associated with any breakup (or even simply renewed fears of its demise) could cause capital flows to reverse," Capital Economists said in a research note. "Nonetheless, pinning down the timing of such events is clearly very difficult and, in the meantime, rapid capital inflows threaten to destabilize the region."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 05-03-12 1241GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120305e835000up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
