\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens On Government  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 21:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real exits active trading at BRL1.7278 Vs BRL1.7112 Thursday\par \par \uc2 --Government slaps taxes on inflows Thursday, buys dollars Friday\par \par \uc2 --Interest rates driver behind year-to-date gains for Brazil real\par \par \uc2 (Updates with comments from economist, trader in paragraphs 10-12)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Tom Murphy \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real ended active trading weaker against the U.S. dollar Friday in the wake of persistent government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the past two sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real ended regular trading at BRL1.7278 to the dollar, weaker than late Thursday's BRL1.7112, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early Thursday, Brazil's government announced a 6.0% IOF financial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tax on foreign loans carrying three-year terms or more. Later Thursday, the government slapped a 6.0% IOF tax on trade finance lines, offered by Brazilian banks, with terms of one year or longer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian officials have been explicit in expressing their concern over the appreciation of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar this year. The real has gained about 10% against the dollar since the beginning of 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to Finance Minister Guido Mantega in recent interviews and statements, the strong real is hurting Brazilian exporters and manufacturers. In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires last week, Mantega said the Brazilian government was prepared to use "a large arsenal of weapons" to fight what he called an "ongoing global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent months, higher IOF taxes on various forms of investment inflows have shown themselves to be one of the main weapons in the government's arsenal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another weapon, frequent purchases of dollars from the spot market, was used aggressively Friday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought unspecified volumes of U.S. dollars at two separate snap auctions Friday, further weakening the real.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deeper problem for policy makers, however, is continued high Brazilian interest rates. Brazil's Selic base interest rate is a towering 10.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While most economists expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cuts to the base rate in the next few months, the rate reductions are unlikely to have much impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even at 8.0%, Brazil's base rate would still encourage the carry trade," said University of Sao Paulo economist Manuel Enriquez Garcia. The so-called carry trade refers to investors who borrow in markets like Japan and the U.S., where interest rates are near zero, and then invest in emerging market countries, such as Brazil, where returns are high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This means we are going to have to live with a strong Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the next couple of years," said Garcia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader, who wished not to be named, said, "The carry trade has been the predominant factor in the market in recent weeks. That's not going to end."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given such underlying factors, tax increases and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market are relatively innocuous measures, according to analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The authorities seem determined to escalate the level of direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," they said in a research note. "But the effects of these types of measures tends to erode over time as financial/capital flows migrate to areas that are less regulated/taxed or restricted."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-07090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 02-03-12 2035GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120302e8320013d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
