\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil's Real Erases Losses As Minister Threatens More  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Cowley   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 15:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed course Thursday midmorning and gained against the dollar, even as Finance Minister Guido Mantega promised more steps to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too fast.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, the government extended a 6.0% tax on short-term foreign loans to loans of up to three years, it said through a decree by President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 . Previously, the financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  tax, or IOF, was charged only on loans of two years or less.\par \par \uc2 Mantega said there was cheap credit and excess liquidity in global markets, which is working its way into Brazil. The government will continue to take measures to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains, Mantega told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts, however, shrugged off this particular measure, although they expressed concern about the threat of more action down the line.\par \par \uc2 "We don't believe this will have a very significant impact in the market but, most importantly, this is a strong signal that the government is not comfortable with the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels and aware and not comfortable with the inflows that are not aimed for investment, and this means that we cannot rule out the possibility of new changes soon," said one trader, in a note.\par \par \uc2 The measure is part of an explicit government effort to arrest the appreciation of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar, and followed two significant \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. So far this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained about 10% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 In this first assault on the key barrier of BRL1.70 per dollar, the government has been successful. After dipping below that level early Wednesday, at BRL1.6902, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost ground sharply against the dollar, and that continued Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 The real weakened to BRL1.7239 per dollar early Thursday, versus a late Wednesday fix of BRL1.7141, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased those losses midmorning, and ended up pushing stronger, to BRL1.7133 at 1417 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Still, many analysts are skeptical that Brazil will be able to stem the tide. This week's move by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide banks with unlimited funding at rock-bottom rates through its Long Term Refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the latest in a series of measures taken in developed countries to inject vast amounts of capital into their stricken financial systems.\par \par \uc2 Some of that money is finding its way into countries such as Brazil, which has a relatively free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stronger growth prospects. Barring a major crisis, many believe the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to gain against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 There was some relief that the government did not increase the IOF tax on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives introduced last July, which currently stands at 1%, but can be raised as high as 25%, said RBS Securities, in a note. Nor did the government reintroduce the IOF tax on foreign inflows into equities that was removed in December 2011, it said.\par \par \uc2 "But for sure, the government is showing its hand and considering the LTRO results and potential inflows to come out of improvement in global liquidity and risk appetite, no doubt more adjustments are ready to go if necessary," \uc2 RBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 01, 2012 09:29 ET (14:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120301e831004rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
