\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil's real gains despite capital controls\b0\par\par\uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 23:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Brazil widens scope of foreign-loans tax to stem inflows\par \par \uc2  * Mexico peso strengthens 1.1 pct; Chile peso slips 0.4 pct \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2  By Guillermo Parra-Bernal and Rachel Uranga\par \par \uc2 SAO\uc2  PAULO/MEXICO CITY, March 1 (Reuters) - Brazil's real climbed on Thursday despite the country's latest capital controls. It was the opposite of the desired effect, coming on the same day that Brazil's president lashed out at U.S. and European monetary policies she blames for inflating the real's value.\par \par \uc2  Brazilian authorities widened the scope of a tax on corporate foreign borrowing, and the move fanned speculation that Latin America's largest economy will fight capital inflows more vigorously.\par \par \uc2  Analysts, however, doubt new measures will be enough to halt the real's advance.\par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real gained 0.47 percent to 1.7120 per dollar, hovering near the 1.70-per-dollar mark the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen defending.\par \par \uc2  "You introduce these measures, and people just back away for a little bit. But in the end, everyone knows there are a lot of flows, and it's inevitable that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will break that" level of 1.70, said Italo Lombardi, a Latin American economist from Standard Chartered Bank in New York.      \par \par \uc2  Brazil's so-called IOF tax was extended to foreign loans with maturities of up to three years from two years previously.\par \par \uc2  BACKLASH AGAINST 'CHEAP MONEY'\par \par \uc2  The move comes on the seventh consecutive day of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchasing dollars in the spot market and on top of  Wednesday's reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot discount the possibility of additional measures being announced to slow \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to threaten breaking below (the 1.70-real threshold)  on a sustained basis," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging markets research at Royal Bank of Canada Securities in Toronto.\par \par \uc2  Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff has even stepped in with a high-profile speech that slammed rich nations for unleashing a "tsunami" of cheap money that was "cannibalizing" poorer countries. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other countries' efforts to spur their economies with low interest rates and cheap loans have fueled gains in the real, which has appreciated more than 8 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors seeking returns higher than those found in developed countries are pouring more money into Brazil and in the opinion of government officials,  hurting local manufacturers by making the country's exports more expensive overseas. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's effort highlights concerns that short-term dollar flows are coming through disguised as intercompany loans, said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, a strategist with Royal Bank of Scotland Securities. She said the move may do little to arrest the real's rally, since many companies borrow abroad for longer than three years.\par \par \uc2  A report by local newspaper Valor Econ\u244\'00\'F4mico, citing sources familiar with the situation, said government officials are mulling a potential tax on foreign direct investment inflows while they remain in cash. The report said that the tax could be refunded once the funds are applied.\par \par \uc2  According to Valor, the government is worried that a meaningful portion of direct investment, the type of capital spending used for productive purposes, is parked in Brazil to earn returns on government debt - which pays one of the world's highest borrowing costs. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Guido Mantega, speaking at a news conference in Brasilia, denied there were plans to tax foreign direct investment. The levy "will continue as it is," he said.\par \par \uc2  "Taxing FDI inflows will be a very risky move, given FDI is seen as helping to improve Brazil's competitiveness, productivity and capital stock, so they will have to tread lightly here," Chamie said.\par \par \uc2   In the year through Feb. 24, U.S. dollar inflows\par \par \uc2 into Brazil totaled $12.533 billion. Much of that money has flowed into the nation's stock market, helping boost the nation's benchmark Bovespa stock index by 16 percent in the same period.\par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's increasing use of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls has spooked some investors.\par \par \uc2  "If I am bullish on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I am bullish on Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I would much rather play Mexico than \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  because there is no fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Lombardi said. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso gained 1.05 percent to 12.7270 per dollar, while the Chilean peso weakened 0.35 percent to bid at 481.50.  \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Guillermo Parra-Bernal in Sao Paulo and Rachel Uranga in Mexico City; Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | ABX | BNX | FUN | U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120301e831000va\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
