\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  Drags Real To Weaker Close\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Februar 2012\par 20:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real closes weaker as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice during session\par \par \uc2 --Government "always evaluating" measures to contain sharp swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL1.7141 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 By Jeff Fick and Luciana Magalhaes\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed weaker Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market twice, reinforcing the government's desire to protect exporters and manufacturers by curtailing the real's nearly 10% gain against the U.S. dollar so far in 2012.\par \par \uc2 "The market is testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  practically every day," said Clodoir Vieira, an economist at Sao Paulo brokerage house \uc2 Souza Barros\uc2 . "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is giving a clear signal that the floor for the real is about BRL1.70 to BRL1.71 to the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.7141 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was weaker from Tuesday's close at BRL1.6992 to the greenback, and well off the opening quote of BRL1.6902 that triggered action from the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An improving outlook for global economic growth and continued ample liquidity in financial markets have once again opened the door for a flood of investment inflows into Latin America's largest economy. Brazil's towering interest rates also continue to make the country a popular destination for investors seeking higher rates of return, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The latest fuel came after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  granted EUR530 billion in inexpensive three-year loans to 800 banks as the single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc continues to struggle with its ongoing debt crisis. The latest wave of loans followed December's EUR489 billion in lending. The ECB aims to avoid an escalating crisis as banks struggle to pay off maturing debts and also to mitigate a sharp pullback in bank lending to customers across ailing European economies.\par \par \uc2 The real has also strengthened as local companies tap overseas debt markets with a series of bond issues.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government is "always evaluating" measures to curb the real's appreciation against the U.S. dollar and there will be a greater chance of action following any sharp gain by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a person familiar with the government's strategy said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its recent string of aggressive actions to contain the real's nearly 10% gain against the U.S. dollar. The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice early in the session after the real opened at BRL1.6902 to the dollar, below the psychologically important BRL1.70 mark that many traders consider the government's "line in the sand."\par \par \uc2 In the first move, the bank sold $1.52 billion worth of so-called reverse swap contracts at two maturities. The actions, which allow investors to swap \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions for government paper linked to interest rates, typically support the dollar by removing Brazilian real exposure from the market. The auctions were used extensively in late 2005 and early 2006 before market interest in the swaps waned. The reverse swaps have been used infrequently since then.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also purchased an undisclosed volume of dollars from the spot market at a snap auction Wednesday, paying BRL1.7032 to the dollar. The real weakened sharply after the two moves, climbing back above the key BRL1.70 mark to trade midafternoon at BRL1.7166 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more aggressive than we would normally expect by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tony Volpon, head of emerging market research at \uc2 Nomura Securities International\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Volpon added that Brazil's government, from now on, may promote a stronger correlation between exchange rates and interest rates. In congressional testimony Tuesday, Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Alexandre Tombini once again strongly hinted at more interest-rate cuts to come.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Selic base rate is currently 10.5%. Volpon said the rate could fall as low as 8.5%. Lower interest rates could help arrest the appreciation of the real by making \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  less attractive as an investment venue.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  also once again ignited market worries regarding sterner \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, such as the taxes Brazil's government slapped on some incoming investments in 2010 and 2011.\par \par \uc2 Last week, in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said the government has "a large arsenal of instruments" that it will use "to prevent an excessive appreciation of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\u