\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil's Tombini Expects 2012 Current Account Deficit Of $75 Bln\b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2012\par 16:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2012 10:57 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Tombini: \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Have Been Useful\par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2012 10:57 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Tombini: \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Have Smoothed \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movements\par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2012 13:28 EDT DJ  2nd UPDATE: Brazil's Tombini Says Inflation To Continue To Slow\par \par \uc2 --Slowdown in inflation will come despite accelerating economic growth over course of year, Tombini says\par \par \uc2 --Also says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to step in and keep real from strengthening too quickly against dollar\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in sixth paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Price increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to slow over the course of 2012, getting closer to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal of 4.5%, Alexandre Tombini, president of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown in inflation will come despite accelerating economic growth over the course of the year, with gross domestic product growing more in the second half than in the first, Tombini told a Senate committee.\par \par \uc2 Economic policies over the past years have set the stage for inflation to slow, and for the neutral interest rate in Brazil to decline.\par \par \uc2 More recently, efforts by central banks around the world to increase liquidity in financial markets have eased investors' aversion to risk and spurred the flow of capital into emerging-market economies, Tombini said.\par \par \uc2 "People are more willing to risk their money," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to step in and keep the real from strengthening too quickly against the dollar, Tombini said. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been useful for smoothing out the fluctuations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 The neutral interest rate in Brazil has declined in recent years, as the government has maintained a policy of fiscal responsibility and inflation goals have been met, Tombini said. The rate is expected to continue to decline in coming years, he added.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeffrey T. Lewis, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 61 9278 5267; jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 28, 2012 13:28 ET (18:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120228e82s004gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
