\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Gains,Limited By Government- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Speculation\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2012\par 19:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real strengthened against the U.S. dollar Friday on progress in the euro-zone crisis and on the outlook for continued investment inflows to the Latin American country, but gains were limited on speculation that the government will be more aggressive in containing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.7040 to the dollar, stronger than the previous close at BRL1.7094, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 The Greek Cabinet on Friday approved a EUR100 billion debt write-down for the country as part of a new multibillion-euro rescue plan Greece is seeking from its international creditors. European Union leaders will meet in less than a week to give final approval for the deal.\par \par \uc2 The bailout "doesn't solve the problem, but it does provide some relief," said Bruno Lavieri, an economist at the Tendencias consulting firm. "Today our markets accompanied the international scenario, while here the government made it clear that the dollar won't go below 1.70 without the government stepping in with heavy measures."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out two purchases on the spot market toward the end of the trading day Friday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to strengthen past 1.70 for a second consecutive day.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, expectations are for continued inflows as investors in developed economies take advantage of low rates at home to borrow and invest abroad. Domestic companies are also seeking financing as markets open up again after last year's cautious tone due to European woes.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Getulio Vargas Foundation helped undergird that optimism, reporting that consumer confidence rose in February, rebounding from a January decline.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's high interest rates and relatively stable growth make it an attractive destination, especially after the lifting of some measures by the government to limit investment that were put in place last year.\par \par \uc2 But the government could put some of those same measures back in place as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens past 1.70 to the dollar, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to convey the message that it doesn't feel comfortable with sub-1.70 levels for the BRL, at least not with such a rapid move this year," \uc2 Banco Santander\uc2  economists wrote in a Friday note. "Going forward, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will very likely have to be much more assertive in the spot market, buying large amounts to sterilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and give the market the signal that it is willing to tackle rapid appreciation of the BRL."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the real strengthened past BRL1.69 to the dollar, with a sale of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and a spot auction to purchase dollars.\par \par \uc2 -By Paulo Winterstein, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7073; paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com [ 24-02-12 1854GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120224e82o0012r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
