\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  weakens as Greek bailout falters\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2012\par 23:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Worries about Greek debt hit risk appetite\par \par \uc2  * Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slips 0.34 pct, Mexico's peso down 1.12 pct \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2  By Lorena Segura and Rachel Uranga\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Feb 10 (Reuters) - Brazil's real and Mexico's peso suffered their worst week in two months, as renewed worries that Greece could fail to meet debt payments hit global markets, while revived \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics weighed in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened sharply on Friday after the leader of a party in Greece's ruling coalition refused to back a 130-billion-euro bailout agreement.\par \par \uc2  The shock, after days of building expectations that Greece was nearing a deal to pass painful austerity measures, played into concerns that a rally in risk assets this year has gone too far, too fast. \par \par \uc2  There could be more profit taking next week given signs Europe is struggling to agree on terms for a second bailout deal for Greece, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Still, many expect further gains in the coming months as a boost in liquidity from central banks eases fears that Greece's troubles could spark another financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think we have seen the end of the rally in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or rates, if the real motivator is external liquidity conditions," said Joel Virgin, an analyst at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 -unit Banamex in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2  In December, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided emergency three-year funding for banks and last month the U.S. Federal Reserve said it envisions exceptionally low interest rates through late 2014. Low interest rates in major economies drive investors to seek higher yields in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  The real  dipped 0.34 percent to bid at 1.7266 per U.S. dollar, pulling further away from the key 1.70-level that many think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government may try to defend with more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or capital controls.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  threw its weight into the spot market on Monday for the first time since September in a bid to limit gains in the real, which has surged so far this year and is threatening the competitiveness of local exporters. \par \par \uc2  Flows to Brazil are being driven by a spate of corporate dollar bond sales, including Brazil's biggest ever by oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , strong foreign investment in infrastructure projects, and a renewal of global interest in the country's stock market.  \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso lost 1.12 percent to 12.82 per dollar. Mexico's peso is the most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, allowing investors to easily bet on losses when global risk appetite sours.  \par \par \uc2  "It's not surprising that we are seeing this weakness, it's expected," said Mauro Roca, an emerging markets strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York. "The question going forward is going to be how long is it going to take for a solution. Then we will have more solid ground for a sustained rally."  \par \par \uc2  Both the real and the peso have gained around 8 percent this year. They clocked their first weekly losses after surging in the first five weeks of the year and posting their strongest period since the rebound from the global credit crisis in early 2009. The real lost 0.6 percent this week while the Mexican peso shed about 1.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Emerging markets rallied this year on improved economic data in China and the United States, key Latin American trading partners, and renewed hope that the euro zone debt crisis could be stabilizing.\par \par \uc2  Euro zone officials say a bailout package must be agreed with Greece and approved by the European Union, the International Monetary Fund and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Feb. 15 to avoid a chaotic default that may threaten the global economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Chile's peso bid 0.77 percent weaker to end at 478.70 per dollar, after hitting a five-month high during the last session. \par \par \uc2 (Writing By Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120213e82a001me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
