\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mixed; Brazil real wilts on cenbank threat\b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2012\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Traders bet on further \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures\par \par \uc2  * Chile's peso ends at 5-month high on Greece deal\par \par \uc2  * Brazil's real slips 0.2 pct, Mexico's peso up 0.32 pct \par \par \uc2 (Recasts throughout; adds closing prices)\par \par \uc2  By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Brazil's real slipped on Thursday as traders poised for another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Chile's peso firmed to a five-month high, driven by optimism that Greece will get its next bailout deal.\par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  threw its weight into the spot market on Monday for the first time since September in a bid to limit gains in the real, which has surged so far this year and is again threatening the competitiveness of local exporters.\par \par \uc2  The real  dipped 0.2 percent to 1.7207 per U.S. dollar, pulling away from the key 1.70-level that many think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government may try to defend with more interevention or capital controls.\par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not bought dollars since Monday, Joao Medeiros, head of trading at Brazilian brokerage Pioneer, said traders moved to the sidelines on expectations that policy makers could enter the market around the 1.7120 level.\par \par \uc2  "That's it for free trading," Medeiros said.\par \par \uc2  Still, analysts think Brazil will be unable to do much more than slow the real's advance due to a tide of dollar flows washing into Latin America's top economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts have been repeatedly deployed in recent years around the region.\par \par \uc2  Flows are being driven by a spate of corporate dollar bond sales, including Brazil's biggest ever by \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , strong foreign investment in infrastructure projects, and a renewal of global interest in Brazil's stock market.\par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of flow from corporates, which will come anyway. In a way there's too many dollars in the market right now," said Pedro Tuesta, Latin America analyst with 4Cast Inc in Washington.\par \par \uc2  The real could get a bigger shock if the government revives a tax on foreign investment in the stock market, which was removed last year after global markets swooned.\par \par \uc2  But improving economic data from the United States and China, Latin America's top trading partners, as well as expectations that central banks in Europe and the United States will keep interest rates low for years to come, has renewed appetite for riskier assets across the board.\par \par \uc2  Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were among the top emerging market assets during the first five weeks of 2012, when they posted their biggest gains since a rebound from the credit crisis in early 2009. \par \par \uc2  Worries about Greece and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts cooled the advance this week, but demand for riskier assets was lifted on Thursday after Greek leaders clinched an austerity deal on reforms needed to secure a second international bailout and avoid a disorderly default. \par \par \uc2  Chile's peso bid 0.55 percent stronger to end at 475.00 per dollar, a five-month high.\par \par \uc2  Following the peso's recent sharp gains, traders say if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens beyond 470 per dollar, Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start voicing concern about the exchange rate. But dollar purchases are seen as unlikely unless the peso breaches at least the 460 barrier and possibly even lower.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's peso firmed 0.32 percent to 12.6750 per dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities are likely welcoming recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that could help cool inflation pressures.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's annual inflation rate pierced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4 percent ceiling for the first time in over a year in January.\par \par \uc2  Investors sought protection in case consumer prices keep rising by piling into Mexican inflation-linked bonds, or Udibonos, pushing prices upward and yields lower.\par \par \uc2  The yield on the December 2014 Udibono bid down almost 9 basis points to 1.28 percent. Strategists at Banorte project its yield could fall to a key support level at 1.20 percent and may break even lower to 1.07 percent. \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Helio Barboza in Sao Paulo and Rachel Uranga in Mexico City; Editing by Gary Crosse)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120212e829000j9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
