\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threat Holds Back  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real -Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2012\par 14:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:12 (Dow Jones) Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained substantial ground against the US dollar to start 2012 as Greece moves toward a debt deal, but concerns about government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weighing on the real, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. The real strengthened past the BRL1.72 mark early in the session amid a positive outlook for Greece's debt restructuring before later weakening. "Progress continues to be held back by the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but we believe that in the current global environment \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only slow down the move," Citi says. Market players on lookout for possible spot-market dollar purchases by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:05 (Dow Jones) \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) January same-store sales do have some skewing that affects how different regions of the world did last month versus a year earlier. The US reported another stout month of growth at 7.8%, but January 2011's increase was a modest 3.1%. Meanwhile, Europe slowed down to 4% after December's 10.8% year-on-year surge. But SSS climbed 7% last January. Globally, MCD's SSS rose 6.7%, above the company's forecast made several weeks ago and marking the 5th-straight month they topped 5%. MCD shares are up 0.6% premarket at $101.50, off slightly from the all-time high set last month. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:51 (Dow Jones) If US regulatory changes to money-market funds end up near what reports have indicated the past day, Sandler O'Neill says, "While the regulatory pressures are a clear negative, it is likely to be manageable for the trust banks. First, money-market fees make up a relatively small portion of trust-bank revenues, especially since the trust banks are already waiving approximately half of money-market fees due to the current low-interest-rate environment. Second, change creates opportunity and the trust banks have other cash-management products and services to offer their primarily institutional money-market fund clients if regulatory changes make the money-market funds less attractive to clients, or economically unattractive to operate." (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:45 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Time Warner\uc2 's (TWX) 4Q earnings show that recent deals struck with distributors like Netflix (NFLX) and Hulu are paying off. Revenue from filmed entertainment gained 7% to $3.9B in 4Q, partly on the back of such deals. No wonder content peers like \uc2 Viacom\uc2  (VIA) are pursuing similar deals. In the broadcasting division, advertising revenue improved modestly, thanks partly to growth abroad. That's not a sign that the ad business is healthy across the board: ad revenue from publishing dropped off 1% in yet another sign advertisers are eschewing print. TWX 4.3% higher premarket at $39.72. (william.launder@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:38 (Dow Jones) Investors seem more focused on OpenTable's (OPEN) 4Q EPS beat in premarket trading, but Oppenheimer is looking ahead as it downgrades the online reservation concern to perform on valuation concerns. While noting margins helped EPS last quarter, "OPEN expects weaker 1Q revenues on tough US comparisons, reduced international dinner pricing and meaningfully higher 1Q expenses." Meanwhile, Benchmark says, "Restaurant and diner growth accelerated in North America" last quarter. And Collins Stewart muses the "strong 4Q proves investor concerns are overblown." OPEN is up 4% premarket at $53.51 but remains down 40% from a year ago. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:34 (Dow Jones) United Continental's (UAL) revenue performance improves in January after a weak end to the year, with consolidated PRASM up an estimated 8.5%-9.5% last month. Just as well, with guidance for fuel expense creeping up to an average of $3.32/gallon for 1Q, 5c more than indicated two weeks ago. Domestic load factor rose in January but, as flagged by advance bookings, dipped on flights to Europe and Asia. Cargo traffic was also down 12%. (doug.cameron@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 (Dow Jones) US stock futures tilted slightly higher, with the gaze still fixed upon a hopeful mood in Europe, reflected in a stronger euro and market gains in Paris, Frankfurt and London. Judging by the euro's advance lately, it seems as if investors think a Greek deal with its creditors and satisfying the troika's demands fixes everything. Presumptuous, at best. Nothing notable on the economic data calendar. Cisco reports F2Q results after the close. EUR/USD teasing toward the $1.33 level, recently at $1.3273. Expect another day of blather and equivocation out of the EU. S&P futures up less than a point, 10-yr note lower, yield at 1.99%. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:15 (Dow Jones) Showing just how far behind Sprint (S) is from its larger US wireless-service rivals, the company saw 1.8M iPhone sales in 4Q, the first period S was able to offer the Apple (AAPL) devices. That pales in comparison to the 4.3M sold by \uc2 Verizon Wireless\uc2  and 7.6M logged by original iPhone purveyor \uc2 AT&T\uc2  (T). Still, 40% of S' iPhone sales were by new customers, helping the company report a rare gain in postpaid customers, 161K. Shares are up 3.3% premarket at $2.53 after reported better-than-expected overall 4Q results, but the stock is down 43% the past year. (greg.bensinger@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:09 (Dow Jones) After easily beating analysts' 4Q EPS targets, \uc2 Time Warner\uc2  (TWX) is looking for further robust growth in 2012. It sees a low-double-digit percentage gain in EPS from last year's $2.89 while analysts--who had last projected $2.82 for 2011--anticipated a 12% increase for 2012 to $3.16. But that view is now just 9.2% above last year's actual profit following the 4Q beat, which was led by sharp profit growth at TWX's networks and publishing businesses. The company is also boosting its dividend 11%. So after a 35% surge in TWX shares the past 4 months, chances are the run is going to continue today. The stock, which is near 4 1/2-year highs, is inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:08 (Dow Jones) Staples' (SPLS) big red easy button gets a digital reboot. The office supplier, which has the biggest e-commerce site on the web behind Amazon (AMZN), launches a tablet-specific online store -- see it at t.staples.com -- that sticks to large, easy to tap icons and allows customers to get products in their carts with in just a couple steps. It was already making a tablet-selling push in brick-and-mortar stores, to give hot items like tablets more exposure. With a new "e-commerce innovation center" slated to open in May, look for similar developments to come. (joan.solsman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:54 (Dow Jones) CVS says its 1Q EPS should be 3c higher than it otherwise would have thanks to rival drug-store chain \uc2 Walgreen's\uc2  (WAG) ongoing contract-renewal spat with pharmacy-benefits manager \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  (ESRX). Business at WAG took a hit in January as it was the first month of no deal between the two, forcing many ESRX customers to go elsewhere to fill prescriptions. CVS' 1Q EPS forecast of 61-63c compares with a consensus 61c view of analysts surveyed by Thomson Reuters. Shares, inactive premarket, are up 29% the past year and are just below all-time highs. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:46 (Dow Jones) Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are expecting further government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, according to a dealer at a major bank. The timing depends on events in Greece. "If there is good news from Greece today about a debt agreement, then the Brazilian real is likely to gain," the dealer says. That, in turn, would probably bring government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the unwanted appreciation as a strong real hurts exporters and manufacturers. The dealer says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars from the market "or more drastic measures, such as new taxes on dollar inflows." (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-08-12 0812ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120208e8280005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
