\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Brazil Real May See Little Support Beyond 1.7/USD\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2012\par 12:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 6:49 (Dow Jones) The external backdrop is unlikely to support appreciation of Brazil's real way beyond 1.7/USD short term, at least not until Greece's debt negotiations are resolved, says Santander Economico. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  slows the appreciation trend, but "generally had a limited impact over the medium term," firm says. Around the 1.9-2.0 level, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to become quite nervous about a disorderly move in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and its impact on inflation expectations; in contrast, the government has shown great aversion to sharp strengthening moves in the BRL, Santander Economico says, noting BRL's unlikely to break out from the 1.7-1.9 range. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:48 (Dow Jones) US stock-index futures edge down, falling less than Europe, as things in Greece remain unresolved. As such, markets are choosing to tread water so long as direction on where talks are headed remains cloudy. There's little notable on the US economic-data front Tuesday while this morning's corporate reports include \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  (KO) and ag-equipment maker AGCO. Dow-industrial futures are down 19 points while the S&P 500 slides 3. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:42 (Dow Jones) A "challenging" F1Q has helped prompt \uc2 Emerson Electric\uc2  (EMR) to cut its targets for the year amid "deterioration in the European economy and mixed global economic indicators." The maker of items such as factory-automation equipment and software and components for appliances will detail its outlook for 2012 and the next 3 years at its annual investor conference next week. But meanwhile, EMR lowered its revenue target for this FY by 2 percentage points to 2-4% and slid its EPS view back several cents amid a $300M revenue impact from Thailand's flooding that EMR says should mostly be recorded as sales as the year progresses. Shares, inactive premarket, are up 15% in 2012. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:33 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT), which says it will spend more money this year building out its Canadian presence than in any prior, comes as US discount rival Target (TGT) prepares to make its entrance into the country in 2013. It will open 100-150 stores initially; WMT has about 335 and plans to boost that number to around 375 a year from now. It intends to spend some $750M this year opening and expanding locations. So as both retailers have been struggling to report consistently solid sales growth in the US, the battle for Canada continues to be prepped. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-07-12 0649ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120207e8270003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
