\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Market Central Banks Back To Square One: Fighting  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Prabha Natarajan and Erin McCarthy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2012\par 22:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Five months later, and Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is back to where it was last September: buying dollars to tame the real's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The hectic pace of the January rally in emerging markets has startled many central banks and forced some of them to take immediate measures to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further rapid appreciation and keep their exports competitive.\par \par \uc2 Investors who are already retooling their strategies to incorporate higher projected year-end levels now also have to factor in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the effort by central banks to slow the pace of appreciation. Colombia's has already indicated that it will follow Brazil's lead and take measures to slow the Colombian peso's ascent.\par \par \uc2 Central banks, meanwhile, may feel compelled to shift from their focus on spurring growth to resuming their battle against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. The announcements from Brazil's and Colombia's central banks were seen as signs of the times, with emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  now having recouped much of the losses that arose during last year's global risk fears, which saw many erase between 10% and 22% of their value against the greenback in the second half.\par \par \uc2 "In the short term, investors have to be concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  especially because of the magnitude of the recent rally," said Andres Garcia-Amaya, strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Funds. "It makes governments jittery as it makes exports less competitive."\par \par \uc2 The huge flow of capital into emerging markets this month--$11.3 billion into equity funds and $1.65 billion into bond funds, according to EPFR Global--was primarily stoked by liquidity measures from both the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, as Guillermo Osses, head of emerging markets debt portfolio management at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Global Asset Management, points out, "Investors won't indiscriminately purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," this time, unlike 2010, when a flood of buying forced many central banks to prevent further appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil, certainly, with its high interest rates, has seen a surge of foreign buyers of its local bonds, and now of its equities too as investors seek other ways to gain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. Latin America's largest economy, in a bid to keep its exports competitive and its manufacturing activity buoyant, last week purchased dollars and did so again Monday.\par \par \uc2 Colombia, the resource-rich commodities exporter, is trying to make sure that the flux of foreign capital flowing into the country consists of investments into the economy and not speculative trading. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday it would start to buy a minimum of $20 million daily for at least three months in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 But, for a while, the duo are likely to be the only countries \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asian and European economies have a way to go before they start worrying about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts and investors said.\par \par \uc2 Some investors, however, already are betting that the measures won't halt the overall appreciation trend, and are happy to hold onto \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 "It does not necessarily make me run to the exit," said Alexander Kozhemiakin, portfolio manager at Standish in Boston, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came a bit sooner than he expected, but his firm is currently long the Brazilian real against the Colombian peso. His read: central banks "are not protecting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a certain level; they're smoothing volatility."\par \par \uc2 Others are waiting for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to present an opportunity to go long the Colombia peso, placing bets that it will appreciate.\par \par \uc2 Alex Pigatto of Nomura Securities expects that Colombia's $400 million a month in dollar purchases would fall far short of its expected foreign inflows of $1.2 billion monthly.\par \par \uc2 "We are flat \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  here, but watching for another opportunity to re-enter in long Colombia Peso [trade]," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Prabha Natarajan, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2468; prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com @djfxtrader [ 02-06-12 1634ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120206e8260005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
