\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Economy To Show Slow Recovery In 2012-Industry Group\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2012\par 20:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --CNI projects Brazil's economy to grow by about 3% this year\par \par \uc2 --Tax incentives for industry, and credit and interest rate easing measures may help improve industrial sales locally, economist says\par \par \uc2 --Industry prospects may be hurt by slow demand abroad and a strengthening of the real\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's economy will show a slow recovery this year from a slowdown in the second half of 2011 amid lingering local structural challenges and continued difficulties abroad, officials from the National Confederation of Industries said Monday.\par \par \uc2 The group said the country's industrial sector ended 2011 in a situation of "near paralysis" as local manufacturing struggled to overcome stiff competition and diminished demand from developed economies.\par \par \uc2 "Industry is showing more or less the same level of production as three years ago, before the emergence of the global financial crisis," said CNI chief economist Flavio Castelo Branco.\par \par \uc2 The CNI reported Brazil's use of installed industrial capacity ended December at 81.3%, down on a seasonally adjusted basis from 82.5% in December 2010 and also well below a record high of 83.9% seen in early 2008.\par \par \uc2 Industrial employment, meanwhile, ended the year 1% lower than December 2010, though real industrial sales showed an advance of 3.4% over the same period.\par \par \uc2 Castelo Branco said he expects Brazil's economy to perform slightly better in 2012 than 2011, but "not substantially."\par \par \uc2 The CNI projects Brazil's economy should grow by about 3% this year. According to the group, Brazil's industrial sector accounts for about a quarter of the country's economic growth.\par \par \uc2 A recent slowdown in demand, Castelo Branco said, could help curb recent heavy imports. Tax incentives for industry, and credit and interest rate easing measures promoted by the government could help improve industrial sales locally, he said.\par \par \uc2 Industry's prospects may be hurt however, by still-slow demand abroad and more particularly by a strengthening of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real.\par \par \uc2 "The appreciation of the exchange rate is a problem for the Brazilian economy that brings increased foreign competition, and in the absence of other instruments to improve competitiveness, it tends to remain a problem," the CNI economist said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real has strengthened by about 8% against the dollar since the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week began purchasing dollars in the spot and forward markets for the first time in five months as the government sought ways to weaken the real.\par \par \uc2 The market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade ministry reported the country posted a $1.29 billion foreign trade deficit in January, its first monthly deficit in two years.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 3335-0832; gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 06, 2012 14:33 ET (19:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120206e826006ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
