\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Closes Weaker As  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again\b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2012\par 20:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real backs off after second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in as many sessions\par \par \uc2 --Real closes weaker at BRL1.7212\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars in first spot auction since September\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Paul Kiernan \par   Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Brazil's real finished slightly weaker against the dollar following choppy trading Monday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market for a second consecutive session, further crediting the view that authorities are drawing a so-called "line in the sand" at current levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.7212 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, compared with BRL1.7158 at Friday's close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday offered to buy dollars in the first spot-market auction in nearly five months shortly after the real touched an intraday high. The move followed an unexpected purchase Friday of dollars for forward delivery, in what many saw as local authorities' return to the policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's real has gained around 8% since the end of 2011 as world financial markets started the new year strong on the back of improving economic data from the U.S. and China. A lull in the European debt crisis also contributed, resulting in favorable financing conditions abroad that several Brazilian companies have taken advantage of to sell bonds at cheaper rates than they can find at home.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fact that companies have sold billions of dollars in overseas bonds since the beginning of the year had led some analysts to hold off on interpreting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday as the start of a trend. Monday's snap auction likely swayed a few more opinions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This should be the answer to the market participants that were wondering if the last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a one-off or a signal that (authorities) will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when they find necessary," one analyst wrote in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's real tends to strengthen when global investor sentiment -- about issues such as the U.S. economy or the European debt crisis -- is optimistic. Perceived low likelihood of a shock in world markets emboldens investors to seek higher returns in traditionally volatile assets like stocks and emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Most investors we talk to are looking to put more cash to work," HSBC Global Research said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, a strong real leaves Brazilian industry at a disadvantage to imported goods and cuts into exporters' profit margins.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's finance ministry and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent years have been sensitive to such concerns. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought $47.91 billion on the spot market and $2.2 billion on the forward market last year, when heavy investment flows pushed the real to its strongest level against the dollar since Brazil adopted a floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime in 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the real became more expensive in recent weeks, market participants wondered aloud at what level the authorities would step in to slow its gains. Finance Minister Guido Mantega said in late January that the government, which unleashed a cocktail of exchange-oriented taxes amid the real's rally last year, planned to stop the real from appreciating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We thought that the closer it got to BRL1.70, the more likely Mantega or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Reginaldo Galhardo, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  director at Sao Paulo's Treviso brokerage. "Apparently that level might be closer to BRL1.72."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; (+55)11-3544-7074; paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 06-02-12 1919GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120206e8260018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
