\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Closes Stronger, Good US News Outweighs  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeffrey T. Lewis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2012\par 19:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real closed stronger against the dollar Friday after good economic news from the U.S. outweighed dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real exited normal trading at BRL1.7158 to the dollar after ending at BRL1.7205 to the dollar Thursday, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The real traded weaker against the dollar at the start of the day amid suspicion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to stop the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening even further.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then validated those suspicions by holding an auction to buy dollars in forward contracts, for delivery March 20, at a rate of BRL1.7383 Brazilian reais. It was the first time in five months the bank moved to weaken the real.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the day, though, good U.S. jobs news overcame the effects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed the real stronger against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The market is watching the whole world, not just Brazil," said Reginaldo Galhardo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo. "There's more enthusiasm about the U.S. economy after the jobs report, and that's helping."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department's January data showed nonfarm payrolls rose 243,000 last month, marking the biggest gain since last April. The jobless rate fell by two-tenths of a point to 8.3%, the lowest it has been since February 2009. Both numbers beat expectations. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expected a slowing to 125,000 jobs from December's 200,000. The unemployment rate had been expected to hold steady at 8.5%.\par \par \uc2 Those figures were good enough to trump the move by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which followed days of statements by Brazilian authorities that they would work to curb the appreciation of the real, which is beginning to hurt Brazilian industry and exporters. In forward auctions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offers to buy contracts for delivery of U.S. dollars at future dates.\par \par \uc2 The real, which closed 2011 at around BRL1.86, has steadily strengthened against the dollar so far this year as concerns about the European debt crisis have subsided and the global economic outlook has improved.\par \par \uc2 A wave of bond issuances by Brazilian companies abroad, and growing world appetite for emerging-market assets, have fueled strong investment flows to Brazil, pushing the real up against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeffrey T. Lewis, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 61 9278 5267; jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Paulo Winterstein and Paul Kiernan in Sao Paulo contributed to this article. [ 03-02-12 1853GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120203e823001bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
