\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Needs Legal Change To Make Real Convertible -Official\b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2012\par 20:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Regulatory changes to make real fully convertible have reached their limit\par \par \uc2 --Laws governing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  need changes to permit more flexibility\par \par \uc2 --Changes to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  law "not on the agenda" at the moment\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeffrey T. Lewis \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRASILIA (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trades as freely as possible right now, given the relevant legal framework, and can only be made fully convertible with legislative action, a member of the government's economic team told \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think that, in practice, we've already advanced all we can from the point of view of regulation," said the person, who asked not to be identified. "To take more steps to make the market more flexible, we start to run up against the question of the law. From the point of view of regulation, what could be done has been done."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current law is restrictive when it comes to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of the country's history, which includes periods of capital flight. Brazilians traditionally sought to buy dollars to protect the value of their savings, especially during periods of high inflation, and the government wanted to deter this to prevent dollar scarcity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent years, the regulations covering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been relaxed somewhat. For example, a plane ticket and a valid U.S. visa are no longer required for individual Brazilians to buy U.S. dollars. Such changes have helped eradicate the black market in dollars, but have reached a limit and the rules can only be further relaxed if the law is changed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There needs to be a political debate, to decide what, if any, changes need to be made, although at the moment such a debate "isn't on the agenda," the person said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The changes necessary to make the real fully convertible would reduce restrictions on who is permitted to trade the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , remove limits on how much can be changed, and reduce the number of transactions that need to be registered with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to the person.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There would be several advantages to permitting the real to trade more freely, chiefly a reduction in the cost of transactions for businesses and tourists alike, while the major drawback would be less government control over the exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Exchange policy would become more challenging for the government," the person said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's weight in global trade isn't at the moment big enough for a fully convertible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be necessary, though in the long term it would be worth it for the country to advance in that direction, the person said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil was responsible for about 1.4% of world trade in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A regionally convertible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be a good first step, given that the real is already much more freely traded with Brazil's neighbors than it is in other parts of the world, the person said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in recent years to slow the appreciation of the real against the U.S. dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on occasion buys dollars to keep the real from strengthening past a certain level, and last July the government imposed a 1% tax on futures contracts for those holding short-dollar positions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tax has been in effect since late last year, although it still hasn't been collected because of the difficulties involved in calculating the amounts owed. The tax will eventually be collected, once the complexities have been ironed out, the person said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeffrey T. Lewis of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; +55 61 9278 5267; jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com [ 27-01-12 1920GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120127e81r0014p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
