\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Latin American Stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Slide In 2011 On Euro Zone\b0\par\par\uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2011\par 23:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("Latin American Stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Slide In 2011 On Euro Zone," published at 1702 EST, misstated the 2011 percentage decline of Mexico's IPC stock index in the second paragraph. The correct version follows.)\par \par \uc2 --Stock indexes end 2011 in red, but select consumer plays flourish\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble against the dollar amid heightened risk aversion\par \par \uc2 --Latin American GDP growth seen at 4.3% this year\par \par \uc2 --Europe's debt troubles to continue pressuring assets in 2012\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Amy Guthrie \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Major Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declined in 2011 as investors shunned conventionally risky assets and exporters amid the euro-zone debt crisis and signs of a slowing global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet the region's major economies continued to grow, and with that growth came greater domestic demand, inspiring double-digit gains in shares of select consumer-driven companies, such as Brazilian phone carrier \f28 \uc2 \f2 TIM Participacoes\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Mexican beverage and retail group Femsa, both of which rose close to 40%. Brazil's broader Ibovespa stock index fell 18.1% on the year, while Mexico's IPC index retreated 3.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian economy, Latin America's biggest, is expected to have grown by 3.5% in 2011 while Mexico's is seen having expanded by 3.9%. The U.N. Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean predicts the region as a whole will grow 4.3% this year and 3.7% in 2012, well above the 2.6% forecast for the world next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Latin America is not immune if Europe's troubles spread. European bank problems and fears of a global credit crunch took a high toll on Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during 2011, with the highly liquid Mexican peso ending down 11.5% at MXN13.9725 to the U.S. dollar, its weakest ever year-end close, and the Brazilian real depreciated 11% against the dollar, to BRL1.8625.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Throughout the year we were rudderless, as money managers, economists and the [local] government weren't really able to determine what the outlook would be," said Reinaldo Zakalski, director of BI Asset Management in Sao Paulo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Chilean peso lost more than 10% this year, to CLP519.20/dollar, after having flirted with a three-year high earlier in 2011. The Chilean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's $12 billion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program fattened the nation's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves while also helping tame the peso. A strong peso can cut into the competitiveness of Chilean products abroad, with exports representing roughly 60% of the country's gross domestic product.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Argentine peso finished at ARS4.3040/dollar, bringing its year-to-date loss to 7.6%, while the Colombian peso closed the year at COP1,938.38/dollar for a slighter loss of 0.9%. The Peruvian sol, meanwhile, appreciated 3.92% against the U.S. dollar to end 2011 at PEN2.697.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market watchers expect major Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to remain weak in early 2012 as European governments continue to grapple with heavy debt burdens. That \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness has also generated concerns of inflationary pressures in the near future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in Brazil, for instance, is at a worrisome 6.6%, with the government there pledging to pull it down to 4.5% by early 2013. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in September, selling dollars in order to avoid what officials called "a disorderly market."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexican officials have also instituted a policy to halt the peso's depreciation on days in which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slides 2% against the dollar, although they have not had to use the dollar-sale mechanism since announcing the plan in late November. Several economists believe the Mexican peso's weakness has begun to pass through to consumer prices, although a devastating drought in northern Mexico has also led to sharply higher prices for a number of agricultural goods. Inflation in Mexico is at 3.85%, near the 4% top of the government's target range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Throughout Latin America, caution led equity investors this year to bulk up on shares of defensive plays--such as phone companies, utilities, makers of paper goods and beverage firms--while reducing their exposure to capital-intensive industries such as construction or export-oriented firms such as steelmakers. Shares of Brazilian steelmaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Usinas Siderurgicas de Minas Gerais\f28 \uc2 \f2 , for instance, slid 40%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Colombia, a boom in the mining and oil sectors, combined with robust consumer spending, especially in the housing and automobile sectors, is driving expectations for a 6% expansion in GDP this year. Yet the country's Colcap stock index ended 14% lower, albeit after a 33% year-on-year surge in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chilean economy will likely have expanded by 6.2% this year, yet that country's blue-chip Ipsa stock index lost over 15%. Peru's General Index shed 16.8% despite an expected 6.8% expansion in GDP, and Argentina's benchmark Merval stock index plunged 30% after having surged 52% in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Amy Guthrie, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; (5255) 5980-5177, amy.guthrie@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Paulo Winterstein and Tom Murphy in Brazil, Anthony Esposito in Chile, Ryan Dube in Peru, Dan Molinski in Colombia and Ken Parks in Argentina also contributed to this report. [ 30-12-11 2220GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1027 1852 5047   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111230e7cu000lf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
