\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To Defend BRL1.93-1.95 -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 994 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 13:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:42 (Dow Jones) Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks set to defend the real whenever it weakens towards BRL1.93-1.95/dollar, predicts RBS. It says, "A proactive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and willingness shown to provide liquidity could help cap USD/BRL at current levels especially considering the ample \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tools" the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government have. RBS contends that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain focused in the futures market via swaps to mitigate the impact of the IOF tax on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, which has dried up trading volumes. "Only in the case of disorderly flows would we expect the government to change the regulation on the IOF taxes while dollar sales in spot seem for now still a long way out." (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:35 (Dow Jones) Citi makes across-the-board cuts to stock-price targets on REITs in the wake of reduced market multiples. But it sees positives. For one, Citi says, "Concern over apartment fundamentals is overblown...and has created an opportunity to purchase stocks with attractive growth prospects and strong balance sheets at substantial discounts to" net asset value. Those "substantial discounts" were also said of many retail REITs as it upgraded Kimco (KIM) and Alexandria (ARE) to buy. Meanwhile, the office and industrial space is "sluggish" and Citi remains overweight on lodging. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 (Dow Jones) With another slump underway in Europe, US stocks aren't nearly as bad off premarket, though losses are building as declines on the Continent worsen further. The euro isn't diving against the dollar, which has generally been a good thing for US equities. But oil is down another 2% on continued global demand concerns, added to today by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' downbeat assessment of the economy world-wide and its recession talk on the US and Europe. S&P 500 futures are down 8 as the day's major domestic economic news could come from Bernanke's appearance before the Joint Economic Committee. Or it could just be rehash of the FOMC's last meeting, at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist was unveiled. Treasurys are little changed. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:12 (Dow Jones) Citi cuts its newspapers ad-revenue estimates in the wake of \uc2 New York Times Co\uc2 .'s (NYT) recent downbeat view and the investment bank's "channel checks suggest[ing] overall industry results are pacing at least as poorly as NYT." That's not good news for an industry which has seen ad revenue decline since before the recession and which is in another uncertain economic climate. Meanwhile, Citi notes that for the past year, "same-store-sales growth has accelerated while newspaper retail ad declines have worsened. This decline is meaningful as print retail revenues make up about 50% of industry ad revenues." Stock-price targets on it, McClatchy (MNI) and Gannett (GCI) are also reduced. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:02 (Dow Jones) The US economy will likely slow "to the edge of recession" by early 2012, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says as it downgrades economic-growth expectations globally. The investment bank also sees or Europe falling into recession in 4Q and 2012 growth of just 0.1%. It notes Europe's crisis threatens the US economy via tighter financial conditions, reduced credit availability and weaker growth of US exports to the region. As for America, Goldman cuts its 2012 GDP-growth forecast to 1.4% from 2%, with a mere 0.5% seen for 1Q. (ian.thomson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:52 (Dow Jones) Citi upgrades Exelon (EXC) to buy, saying the largest owner of nuclear-power plants in the US has warmed the investment bank over the past several months "on a variety of structural themes that should play out over the next few years." They include EPA regulations "that should benefit Exelon more most other integrated utilities" in the mid-Atlantic region, its pending purchase of Constellation (CEG) and a dividend yield that is the highest among its peers. Citi boosts its price target on the stock to $48 from $41 while raising EPS forecasts through 2013 for EXC's current \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Shares, inactive premarket, closed Monday at $41.20 and are down just 1.1% this year. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:37 (Dow Jones) AMR shares are unsurprisingly set for a rebound after Monday's collapse, while some analysts are already seeing a possible silver lining from any pre-packaged bankruptcy--something the parent of American Airlines has said it has no intention of pursuing. Bernstein reckons the 460 planes ordered during the summer from Airbus and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  (BA) are safe, and any that AMR elects not to take delivery of will find happy homes elsewhere given pent-up demand for narrowbodies, and probably without the manufacturers having to finance them as they have pledged to for AMR. Shares rose 8.1% after-hours following the 33% slump in regular trading. (doug.cameron@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:18 (Dow Jones) US stock-index futures are lower, though are holding up much better than the 2%-plus declines being posted by major European indexes, many of which have fallen through key psychological levels and are testing support. Today's development on the sovereign-debt front include euro-zone finance ministers again delaying a decision on Greece's next batch of bailout loans, keeping default fears front and center. In the US, a reading on August factor orders is due at 10 am EDT. Dow industrial futures are down 30 points and the S&P 500 is off 2.5. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-04-11 0742ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111004e7a40003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
