\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  In Focus As Real Starts Another Bumpy Ride\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Cowley   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was back in focus Friday morning as traders once again braced for a bumpy ride in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The real weakened early versus the dollar, but then reversed course, as markets remained jittery following Thursday's slump. While the underlying cause for much of the volatility is the financial uncertainty in Europe and the U.S. monetary policy, investors in Brazil are using the opportunity to reshape their portfolios.\par \par \uc2 Traders also have to deal with the prospect of a repeat of the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaken on Thursday. The bank offered $5.6 billion in swap contracts, effectively selling dollars, and sold about half.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come in again with swaps, promising to deliver dollars," said Luiz Carlos Baldan, a director at the Fourtrade brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 At 1241 GMT the real was trading at BRL1.8885 per dollar, stronger than the BRL1.8971 at which it exited trading on Thursday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had touched BRL1.9146 earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners aren't necessarily taking their money out of Brazil, but are changing around their investments, exiting the stock market, hedging their money and buying gold, Baldan said. Otherwise, trade flows are normal, though as expected there are some larger outflows into year-end as companies repatriate profits, he said.\par \par \uc2 RBS Securities said that there's likely to be more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action as it senses "a growing discomfort" among some groups, mainly Asian retail investors, with their exposure to the Brazilian real through packaged investment products.\par \par \uc2 The direct impact of these is on the offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but there is an indirect impact onshore, RBC said. The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has less information about the offshore flows, but the warning signs in the moves in the relationship between the real and the yen are clear, it said.\par \par \uc2 "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain concentrated in [\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's] futures market for now" as Japanese funds start to unwind positions, amid deteriorating onshore liquidity, it said.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, as the real weakens faster than commodities prices, inflation concerns are squarely back in view. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had started cutting rates, but the inflation shock from imported inflation poses a dilemma.\par \par \uc2 "The implications for monetary policy are that unless we see a disruption in global credit markets and a sell-off in commodity prices...then stable interest rates would be the most appropriate policy path," RBS said. Given the uncertainty, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change direction again so quickly, so there's likely to be more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against disorderly outflows, RBS said.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com [ 23-09-11 1254GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument 