\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  says ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; real firms\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 19:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Real strengthens over 2 pct after cenbank comments\par \par \uc2 * Dollars not flowing out of the country, finmin says\par \par \uc2 * Cenbank monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact on inflation\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background, byline)\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb volatility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one day after surprising markets with a move to stop the real from weakening.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to investors in Washington, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, Alexandre Tombini, said Brazilian policy makers want to see where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stabilize "in the next days or weeks" to assess its impact on inflation, which he sees as declining overtime.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the bank will work to avoid excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , providing liquidity to the market whenever necessary, Tombini said, helping the real strengthen more than 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make sure \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets work properly," he said in an event organized by the Brazilian American Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> strengthened to 1.85 per dollar following his comments, after closing near 1.9 per greenback on the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The real had weakened to 1.95 during Thursday trading as investors became more averse to the risk of another global financial crisis, prompting Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell $2.75 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to cushion its fall.\par \par \uc2 That move was designed to provide liquidity in the derivatives market, where investors were quickly turning more bearish on the real, Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega told investors in the same event.\par \par \uc2 "The reason behind the weakening of the real is a sharp adjustment in the derivatives market, not dollar outflows," Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 He also promised the government will maintain its fiscal consolidation plans by cutting operating expenditures in order to make room for infrastructure investment and for further monetary policy easing, especially if the global financial crisis worsens.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian economy had been resisting the global crisis so far, but the effects have started to become visible. Given global constraints, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely revise down, by the end of the month, its 4 percent economic growth forecast for this year, Tombini said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Pedro da Costa; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-CENBANK/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110926e79n001a1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
