\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real, Mexican peso slides; Chile's gains\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 17:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Germany gloom, Japan debt rating cloud world outlook\par \par \uc2 * U.S. data fails to lift Mexico peso as trade weakens\par \par \uc2 * Argentina's peso slides as trade surplus narrows.\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds Argentine surplus, Mexican debt)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened or trimmed gains against the U.S. dollar in late trading on Wednesday as investors judged the prospects for world economic growth to be weak.\par \par \uc2 The declines came as business confidence in Germany, Europe's largest economy, dropped to its lowest level in 14 months and after Moody's Investors Service cut Japan's debt rating to Aa3 from Aa2.\par \par \uc2 The data from Germany does not bode well for investment in the world's second-largest exporter. The Japanese rating cut could boost borrowing costs in the world's third-largest economy, reducing the relative attractiveness of higher-yielding Latin American investments. For more see: [ID:nL5E7JO0XO] [ID:nL4E7JO0DF]\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors hunting higher returns than they can get at home have been a key source of speculative capital in Latin America, helping Brazil's real and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.\par \par \uc2 "The market is very unstable and will likely remain so until we can get some clearer idea of the direction of world growth," said Mario Battistel, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fair Corretora, a Sao Paulo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage.\par \par \uc2 "The short-term outlook is not good, investors are cautious, they see a minefield."\par \par \uc2 The outlook is so negative, Battistel added, that stronger-than-expected U.S. durable goods orders had little or no effect on Wednesday's regional declines. [ID:nN1E77N096]\par \par \uc2 "Frankly, people are waiting for Bernanke to talk on Friday," Battistel said referring to a scheduled address in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke that some expect to include new stimulus measures for the U.S. economy. [ID:nLDE77N0G1]\par \par \uc2 Increased orders for autos, aircraft, washing machines and other expensive, long-lasting manufactured products, under the umbrella term of durable goods, show the U.S. economy has more power to grow than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 Not even Mexico's peso pulled out of its dive in the wake of the U.S. durables data. Mexico gets about 80 percent of its export earnings from the United States, much of it from the sale of big-ticket durable goods.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MXN=D2>, Latin America's most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , weakened 1.24 percent to 12.4665 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mexican local bonds <MX10YT=RR> also plunged, with the 10 year, peso-denominated "bono" falling 2.49 to 104.09 percent of face value, its biggest one-day drop since June 21. The yield rose to 6.12 percent.\par \par \uc2 The country posted a seasonally adjusted trade deficit <MXTBAL=ECI> of $364 million in July, 45 percent more than a year earlier and 18 percent more than in June, the government said on Wednesday. [ID:nEMS3ZP3JU]\par \par \uc2 The non-adjusted deficit was $1.179 billion in July compared with a surplus of $108 million in June.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY>, the region's second-most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , weakened 0.65 percent to 1.6097 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's peso <ARSB=> also dropped. The country's unofficial, or parallel, exchange rate slipped 0.51 percent to 4.385, a record low and its biggest one-day decline in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The country's trade surplus shrank by nearly a quarter to $673 million in July compared with a year earlier as imports outpaced exports. [ID:nN1E77M1MY]. The median estimate in a survey of analysts by Thomson Reuters was for a surplus of $760 million.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's official, \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -controlled rate <ARS=RASL> was unchanged at 4.185 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real and Argentina's peso may be in for more declines. In a note to investors on Wednesday, Flavia Cattan-Naslausky of RBS Capital in Greenwich, Connecticut, recommended selling the Brazilian real and Argentine peso, buying the Chilean and Colombian pesos and maintaining Mexican peso holdings.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real will not strengthen much beyond 1.60 to the the dollar without prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry, Cattan-Naslausky said.\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol <PEN=PE> was little changed from Tuesday at 2.7310 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> gave up some early gains to firm 0.11 percent to 467.70 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP2=STFX> firmed 0.17 percent to 1,786.01 after earlier strengthening as much as 0.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by W Simon and Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110827e78o000f7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
