\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Raymond Colitt   \par \uc2 1,419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 16:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Aug 2 (Reuters) - High-profile scandals in President Dilma Rousseff's government have soured ties with her allies, stalling legislation in Congress and delaying key infrastructure projects before the 2014 World Cup.\par \par \uc2 Other risks include an apparent lack of interest in long-term structural reforms, pressure to heighten public spending, and renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 SCANDALS\par \par \uc2 A corruption scandal that forced the top ranks of the transport ministry to resign is delaying the construction of roads, ports and railways that form part of Rousseff's flagship public works program, PAC. [ID:nN1E7651GA].\par \par \uc2 This could have a broad impact in the real economy, affecting soy exporters facing shipping delays due to poor roads or IT manufacturers waiting for fiber optic cables to launch the assembly of new tablet plants.\par \par \uc2 The latest accusations of kickback schemes will likely also slow other infrastructure projects, including preparations for the World Cup in 2014 and the 2016 Olympics, as government watchdogs step up scrutiny of contracts.\par \par \uc2 "We're reviewing all the projects in the transport ministry -- with a strong magnifying glass," Transport Minister Paulo Passos said.\par \par \uc2 The shake-up also renewed tensions with Rousseff's allies, particularly because she decided to fire many officials before concluding an official inquiry.\par \par \uc2 If Rousseff continues her "cleanup" in other ministries and state agencies, as her aides say she intends to, the career civil servant may boost her popular approval ratings but risks losing support from some allies who expect to get extra "perks" from government contracts and posts.\par \par \uc2 Already her coalition had been plagued by infighting over key government posts and was recently hit by the resignation of her chief of staff, Antonio Palocci, over an ethics scandal [ID:nN08245414].\par \par \uc2 Her refusal to make political appointments to key jobs was one of the factors angering her main ally, the PMDB.\par \par \uc2 LEGISLATIVE AGENDA\par \par \uc2 In an effort to avoid potential blackmailing by allies seeking compensation for support, Rousseff has tried to govern without Congress as much as possible, her aides say.\par \par \uc2 Her agenda in Congress has been stalled for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Reforms to the tax code and the pension system, which investors say are necessary to keep the economy growing, may not be passed for months, if at all. [ID:nN1E75L0H2]\par \par \uc2 Other projects on hold include legislation related to new offshore oil fields, and bills aimed at ensuring that Brazil builds stadiums, airports and other infrastructure that is not only needed for the World Cup and the 2016 Olympics but also to overcome bottlenecks holding back the entire economy.\par \par \uc2 But with the sporting events drawing nearer and a slowing economy exposing glaring problems from unwieldy labor laws to heavy taxes, Rousseff may need to engage Congress to approve structural reforms and emergency bills to speed up government procurement. In doing so, she may have to ease some of the austerity adopted in the first six months of her term.\par \par \uc2 What to watch for:\par \par \uc2 - Signs that Rousseff will loosen spending controls or make appointments to please allies.\par \par \uc2 - A major financial crisis abroad, perhaps in Europe, that would require quick action from Brazil's Congress to prevent economic trouble at home.\par \par \uc2 - New media revelations of scandal among top officials. [20110802 145759 GMT]\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT MEDDLING\par \par \uc2 Rousseff's meddling in state and private companies has weighed on blue chips and helped push down the Bovespa <.BVSP> index by 15 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Evidence that Rousseff helped oust the chief executive of heavyweight iron ore exporter Vale renewed concerns of meddling in strategic industries.\par \par \uc2 The move was intended to get Vale and the mining industry to invest more at home, buying Brazilian, not imported, ships and producing more steel and exporting less iron ore.\par \par \uc2 The government has also pressured state-controlled oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  not to raise domestic gasoline prices, damaging its bottom line. Rousseff has also pushed \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  to cut natural gas prices in order to help lower costs for industries and keep domestic fuel prices at bay. [ID:nN1E75S0FM]\par \par \uc2 A mining bill Rousseff is expected to send to Congress this year would likely reduce the time companies have to develop mines to discourage speculation in mineral properties, and could hike royalties to promote mineral processing.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICY\par \par \uc2 The risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains high, as a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undermining several exporters' international competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 When the real <BRBY><BRL=> hit a 12-year high in July at 1.54 per U.S. dollar, the government imposed a tax on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, the latest and potentially most aggressive measure so far designed to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally [ID:nN1E76Q01I].\par \par \uc2 If that measure proves ineffective, the government could hike the tax rate on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives from currently 1 percent to as much as 25 percent and adopt other measures as well, Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  has said.\par \par \uc2 With consumer price pressure slowly ebbing, the government will rely less on a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to anchor inflation in coming months and may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively.\par \par \uc2 Any fresh measures could hit fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors, while the stronger real can erode profit margins and market share of exporters, particularly manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 AGRICULTURE\par \par \uc2 A legislative proposal changing environmental regulations for farmers passed the lower house of Congress and could become law later this year. While the Forestry Code, which sets the percentage of native forest farmers must preserve, reduces overall environmental liabilities for farmers, they would have to finance costs they have been putting off for years to reforest or invest in protected areas.\par \par \uc2 Apparent disagreement in the government over measures to restrict land purchases by foreigners has renewed concern among investors. The agriculture ministry would like to revise last year's measures to restrict foreign speculative capital but promote investments into production. [ID:nN16140439]\par \par \uc2 MONETARY POLICY\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation is starting to ease but remains very close to the government's upper limit of 6.5 percent. [ID:nN1E76O03S]\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has described rising prices as the biggest policy challenge for her government, aware that inflation tends to hit hardest the poorer classes who form her political base. A sustained rise in prices could result in a significant deterioration of her approval rating.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has extended its cycle of interest rate hikes, raising rates 175 basis points this year to 12.50 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has signaled it may soon end the tightening cycle but markets expect a further hike of 25 basis points.\par \par \uc2 That gives Brazil some of the world's highest rates, which in turn could brake consumer spending. Already there are signs of slowing demand. Sales of packaging material, a key indicator of consumer goods sales, fell 3.6 percent in June from May.\par \par \uc2 Inflation and rising interest rates have also weighed significantly on the stock market this year.\par \par \uc2 PUBLIC ACCOUNTS\par \par \uc2 After a spending boom ahead of the presidential election last October, the government has gradually improved its public finances. The primary budget surplus, which excludes interest payments on debt, jumped to 3.54 percent of GDP for the 12 months through June, up from about 2 percent in mid-2010.\par \par \uc2 But public accounts could worsen in the second half of the year. Much of the surplus has come from non-recurring tax revenue, and slower economic growth means lower tax revenue than expected. The economy grew 7.5 percent in 2010 and is now expected to slip to under 4 percent this year. [ID:nN1E76R0YM]\par \par \uc2 Also, Rousseff is facing rising pressure to increase pork barrel spending after more than 6 months of austerity.\par \par \uc2 What to watch for:\par \par \uc2 - Sliding tax revenue, rising expenditures.\par \par \uc2 - Growing political pressure to hike pork barrel spending.\par \par \uc2 ROUSSEFF'S HEALTH\par \par \uc2 Rousseff's hospitalization and her lengthy recovery from pneumonia in May renewed concerns over her health, which first surfaced in April 2009 when she announced she was being treated for lymphoma. [ID:nN01161483] [ID:nN12259498]\par \par \uc2 She has been given a clean bill of health by doctors, but fresh evidence shows that Rousseff suffers from several other ailments. The 63-year-old trained economist is reported to also suffer from diabetes, hypothyroidism, and occasionally high blood pressure. During her treatment for pneumonia she complained of stomach pains, showed signs of an over-worked liver and had a low white blood cell count.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff's health is on investor radar screens. Were it to worsen, markets could slump due to uncertainty over economic policy under Vice President Michel Temer.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kieran Murray) [20110802 145848 GMT]\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-RISKS/|BRAZIL-RISKS/|chained   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110805e782001qr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
