\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Market Weaker As Concerns About Growth Return\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2011\par 21:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real weakens as investor concerns about global economic growth return\par \par \uc2 --Early session rally on U.S. debt deal fades on weak manufacturing data from China, Europe and U.S.\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has adapted to latest \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, with no lack of liquidity\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL1.5615 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Jeff Fick \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market ended weaker Monday as weak manufacturing data reignited concerns about global economic growth, cutting short an early session rally sparked by a deal to resolve the U.S. debt ceiling debate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real posted early session gains, tracking emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  advances abroad, after U.S. lawmakers reached a deal to raise the debt ceiling and reduce the deficit. The U.S. House and Senate are expected to vote on the measure later Monday. But the euphoria in global financial markets was short-lived.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A lower-than-expected reading in the Institute for Supply Management's July manufacturing index combined with weak output figures from Europe and China to once again showed that global economic growth is shaky, traders said. And despite a resolution on raising the U.S. debt ceiling, credit-rating companies could still decide to downgrade the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The world is really passing through a deceleration," said Alfredo Barbutti, an economist at Sao Paulo trading house BGC Liquidez.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about the fiscal health of lesser European Union members also is returning, said Barbutti. The economist noted that spreads for sovereign bonds issued by Belgium, Italy and Spain were widening to historic highs. The movement shows that the situation in Europe is "deteriorating and could spread" to other countries in the single-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc, Barbutti said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian real exited active trading at BRL1.5615, according to Tullet Prebon via Factset. That was weaker from Friday's close at BRL1.5504 to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Brazil, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders have already adjusted to last week's move to tax short \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -derivatives positions, said Reginaldo Siaca, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at the Advanced brokerage in Sao Paulo. In the immediate aftermath of the measures, "the market was a mess," Siaca said. "Now, everyone has assimilated the changes. But if the government doesn't think the measures are working to its satisfaction, they'll raise the tax."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial-transactions tax is currently 1%, but it could be raised to as much as 25% by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's National Monetary Council.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the prospect of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could weigh on the real in the short term, the broader trend for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains tilted toward appreciation thanks to the dynamics of Latin America's largest economy, said the Brazilian unit of Spain's Banco Santander.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Strong fiscal and external outlooks compared with the advanced world, massive interest-rate differentials and strong [foreign direct investment] inflows should help the [Brazilian real] remain around the BRL1.55 handle," Santander said in a research report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's towering interest rates will likely be the source of most downward pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , thanks to an inflation outlook that remains above the government's target. For a second-consecutive week, economists and analysts raised their 2012 outlook for inflation to 5.3% from 5.28% the previous week, according to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weekly market survey. For 2011, the year-end forecast was maintained at 6.31%. Both estimates are above the government's inflation target of 4.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists also expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to boost the benchmark Selic base interest rate a sixth time in 2011 to 12.75%. Currently, the Selic stands at a towering 12.5%--by far the highest interest rate among the world's major economies. The Selic is expected to be 12.5% by year-end 2012, according to the survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; \par    jeff.fick@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-08-11 1954GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110801e781001cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
