\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:Vale CFO: \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Help Stabilize Strong  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kris Maher   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2011\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Brazilian mining company \uc2 Vale SA\uc2  (VALE, VALE5.BR) expects government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help stabilize the strong Brazilian real, said Guilherme Cavalcanti, the company's chief financial officer, in an interview with The Wall Street Journal on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "We think the exchange rate in Brazil will reach a bottom because the government is starting to bring measures to not let the real appreciate further," Mr. Cavalcanti said.\par \par \uc2 This week, Brazil imposed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures aimed at curbing the strong real, including a 1% financial-transactions tax on short-dollar positions. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil has also been purchasing dollars at spot market auctions to slow the gain of the real, as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded this week at its strongest levels against the dollar in 12 years.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Vale, the world's largest producer and exporter of iron ore, posted another round of strong quarterly profits Thursday on robust demand in Asia. But Mr. Cavalcanti said the real's appreciation is still having "a huge impact" on the company. That is because while Vale's revenue is in dollars, most of its costs for labor and other expenses at its Brazilian mines are in reais.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cavalcanti said he tries to deal with this "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mismatch" with hedging in the futures markets and by keeping a major part of the company's cash position in reais.\par \par \uc2 "My assets tend to be a hedge for my costs," he said. "I can't hedge everything because the amount is too big."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which appreciated 4.2% against the dollar in the second quarter, boosted Vale's costs in the period. At the same time, Vale made $360 million in the second quarter as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate swaps.\par \par \uc2 The strong real is generally a drag on Brazilian exporters, because it makes their products more expensive to foreign buyers. But in Vale's case, the booming market for iron ore has sent the company's profits soaring.\par \par \uc2 Vale reported Thursday that it had a profit of $6.45 billion in the second quarter, a 74% increase from a year earlier. That was down slightly from a record quarterly profit of $6.83 billion in the first quarter, and less than some analysts had forecast.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cavalcanti said Vale expects to continue to benefit from strong demand from China and that iron ore prices will stabilize at current high levels.\par \par \uc2 China accounts for roughly 40% of Vale's shipments of iron ore and iron pellets. Vale said its average sales price for iron ore in the second quarter was $145.30 per metric ton, up 58% from the year-earlier period.\par \par \uc2 "We're not seeing any slowdown from China," Mr. Cavalcanti said, noting China's strong growth in GDP in the second quarter and solid increases in housing starts and sales. He added that Japan's rebuilding efforts following the earthquake earlier this year will provide another source of demand in the region.\par \par \uc2 "For Asia, we are very bullish, and prices will continue to stabilize," Mr. Cavalcanti said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cavalcanti said he is not worried about any potential fallout related to the debt discussions in Washington, in part because the U.S. is not a significant market for the company. In addition, he said he would expect that even if Western countries experienced economic repercussions, China would increase investment in infrastructure to keep its own economy strong.\par \par \uc2 "We think the Asian economy has this capacity to offset crises in the Western world," Mr. Cavalcanti said. [ 07-29-11 1400ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110729e77t000kn\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (brazil near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx20